# Appendix C — The Framebuffer Model

Chapter 3 taught you to light a pixel. This appendix is the model underneath it:
how big the screen is, how a coordinate maps to a pixel, what a color is, and what
happens when you draw off the edge. Every fact here is something the console will
confirm if you type it at the prompt — so it does.

## The screen is a grid you can measure

Do not take the size on faith; ask the console. `pixels-wide` and `pixels-tall`
report the framebuffer's dimensions:

```motoi
(display (list (pixels-wide) (pixels-tall)))
(newline)
```

```
(80 80)
```

The screen is an **80 by 80 grid** — 6,400 pixels. That is the whole canvas.
Coordinates run from `0` to `79` on each axis: `(0, 0)` is the top-left corner,
`(79, 79)` is the bottom-right. There is no pixel `80` — the counting starts at
zero, so the last valid index is one less than the size.

## A pixel is a coordinate and a color

`pset x y c` sets the pixel at column `x`, row `y` to color `c`. `pget x y` reads
the color back. Write one, read it:

```motoi
(pset 3 4 12)
(display (pget 3 4))
(newline)
```

```
12
```

You set `(3, 4)` to color `12` and `pget` returned `12`. That is the whole
framebuffer contract: `pset` writes, `pget` reads, and what you read is exactly
what you wrote. The screen is memory you can see.

## An untouched pixel is color 0

Every pixel you have not written is color `0` — the background. Ask about one you
never set:

```motoi
(display (pget 5 5))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

`0` — the console does not invent a color for a pixel you never touched; it hands
back the honest background. A fresh screen is 6,400 pixels of color `0`, waiting.
Color `0` is the eraser, too: `(pset x y 0)` returns a pixel to the background,
which is exactly how Chapter 16 wiped a pixel to prove the cart carried the real
one. Motoi's palette is **sixteen colors**, numbered `0` through `15`.

## The edge clips — you cannot draw off the screen

What if you write outside the grid? The console does not error and does not grow
the screen; the write simply lands nowhere:

```motoi
(pset 80 80 9)
(display (pget 80 80))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

You asked to set `(80, 80)` — one past the last valid index on each axis — and
reading it back gives `0`, the background. The write clipped. This is a *feature*:
a sprite that walks off the edge does not corrupt anything or crash the console;
its off-screen pixels quietly vanish. You can always draw as if the world were
bigger than the screen, and the framebuffer keeps only the part that fits.

## Filling a block: `fb/rect`

Setting pixels one at a time is fine for a dot; for a block, `fb/rect x y w h c`
fills a rectangle `w` wide and `h` tall, its top-left corner at `(x, y)`:

```motoi
(fb/rect 0 0 4 4 7)
(display (list (pget 0 0) (pget 3 3) (pget 4 4)))
(newline)
```

```
(7 7 0)
```

Read the three probes. `(0, 0)` is `7` — the top-left corner is filled. `(3, 3)`
is `7` — the bottom-right *inside* the rectangle is filled. `(4, 4)` is `0` — one
past the edge, untouched. A `4`-wide rectangle covers columns `0, 1, 2, 3` — four
columns, ending *before* column `4`. The width is a count, not a last-index; the
same zero-based counting that makes the last screen pixel `79`, not `80`.

## The mental model, in one paragraph

The framebuffer is an 80×80 array of color numbers, 0–15, all starting at `0`.
`pset` writes one cell, `pget` reads one, `fb/rect` writes a block. Coordinates
are zero-based, so valid indices are `0`–`79`; anything outside clips away
harmlessly. Nothing here is hidden — every claim on this page is one `pget` away
from being confirmed at the prompt.

